From 32e3bd85a335dd29ceb640c055e343c672d6154e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nova <nova@papi-ux.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 13:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] feat(video): report bounded native packet reception

Nova #442, the client half of a private, negotiated, observation-only
extension paired with Polaris #615. The host offers
x-ss-video.packetReceptionFeedbackVersion:1; the client echoes
x-ml-video.packetReceptionFeedbackVersion:1, exactly once each, only for a
Sunshine host on encrypted control protocol 13 with control v2 enabled,
session-ID connect data present and a monotonic clock. Video is observed
in whichever encryption mode existing policy negotiated; the extension
never requests or changes encryption. Old, unknown, malformed, duplicated
or one-sided peers stream exactly as before.

The video receive thread reads a monotonic microsecond clock immediately
after a positive recvUdpSocket() and keeps the original UDP length. Only
the RTP queue's acceptance branch commits a packet, after queuePacket()
accepted an original (past AES-GCM when video is encrypted) and before
reconstructFrame() can stage, submit or free it. Runts, AES-GCM failures, the pre-decrypt
old-frame filter, duplicates, rejected packets, late parity and
FEC-synthesized packets never count. RtpvAddPacket() keeps its behavior
and RtpvAddObservedPacket() carries the capture.

The collector is fixed memory: counters, the frame range, and the first
accepted packet in each of sixteen 6.25 ms bins, under a short mutex with
no per-packet allocation. The existing periodic control tick takes at
most one snapshot and sends a 52 + 10 * n byte (<= 212) big-endian report
as message 0x7F10, unreliable and unsequenced on the generic channel,
flushed by the existing ping. A failed send drops only that observation.
There is no retry, queue or catch-up.

Clock failure or regression sends one zero-time CLOCK_UNAVAILABLE
sentinel and stops the extension until a new native connection. A
failed or backward socket-read clock counts even when that packet is
never accepted; the receive thread's reads are checked against its own
earlier socket reads and commits, so a read taken before a concurrent
control snapshot is not mistaken for a regression. Once that check
completes, the read also raises (never lowers) the floor that later
snapshots and commits must meet. Long
intervals are INCOMPLETE without samples. Control stop retires the
collector only after joining the loss-stats thread, its sole publisher,
so a snapshot already taken is enqueued before retirement or not at all
and nothing is enqueued after it; destroy clears the collector, so a new
connection starts again at sequence 1 with a new origin.

PltGetMonotonicMicros() reports failure instead of falling back to wall
time. The wire contract is Nova's docs/native-packet-reception-feedback.md.
This is not a packet census, capacity, one-way delay or health signal, and
no client code acts on it.
